      "math_academy_id": "e-14221",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $| 2x+9 | = 15.$",
      "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|2x+9| = 2x+9,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+9| & =15 \\\\ 2x+9 & =15 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2x+9| = -(2x+9),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+9| & =15 \\\\ -(2x+9) & =15 \\\\ -2x-9 & =15 \\\\ -2x & =24 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2} & =\\frac{24}{-2} \\\\ x & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-12$ and $x=3.$",
